      "code_hash": "059746a021d951eea9d6a82c4d64516a",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><h1 id=\"13-plans-meaning-and-proofs\">13. Plans, meaning and proofs</h1>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">The chapters so far were about using the library. If you're the person\nwho has to explain to a reviewer why a \u2705 can be trusted, this part is\nfor you. It starts with the three ideas everything rests on:</span>\n<ol>\n<li>a query is a <strong>plan</strong>, a tree of small operators;</li>\n<li>a plan has a <strong>meaning</strong>, given by an interpreter that serves as the\n   definition of \"correct\";</li>\n<li>a <strong>theorem</strong> is a claim about <em>all</em> inputs, and Lean checks its proof.</li>\n</ol>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">Along the way you'll see why SQL's NULL makes \"obviously true\" claims\nfalse, and why that is exactly the kind of bug proofs catch and tests\nmiss.</span></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
    {
      "id": "bkHC",
      "code_hash": "daddbca9828a2d25742dd45689adf001",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><h2 id=\"a-query-is-a-tree-of-operators\">A query is a tree of operators</h2>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">When you write</span>\n<div class=\"language-sql codehilite\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"k\">SELECT</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">id</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">amount</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">AS</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">doubled</span>\n<span class=\"k\">FROM</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">orders</span>\n<span class=\"k\">WHERE</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">amount</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">10</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">a database does not run the text. It first turns it into a <strong>plan</strong>: a tree\nof small operators, each consuming the rows of its child.\n<a href=\"https://substrait.io\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Substrait</a> is an open standard for such plans, so\nengines like DataFusion, DuckDB and Polars can exchange them. In this\nproject, DataFusion writes the plan and Lean reads it.</span></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
    {
      "id": "lEQa",
      "code_hash": "69b4e1d803eb1317e752d7a57b01112f",
