    {
      "id": "PKri",
      "code_hash": "63da4cfafbb5357fc55de20a1885c514",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><span class=\"paragraph\">Two Substrait quirks worth knowing, because the Lean model copies them:</span>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Columns are numbered, not named.</strong> <code>amount</code> is just \"column 2\"\n  (counting from 0). In Lean this is written <code>$2</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Project only <em>adds</em> columns.</strong> It appends <code>amount * 2</code> as column 3; a\n  separate <strong>Emit</strong> step then keeps columns 0 and 3. So <code>SELECT</code> is\n  really \"Project, then Emit\".</li>\n</ul>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">In Lean, a plan is a value of the type <code>Rel</code>, with one case per operator.\nHere is the start of its definition, from <code>SubstraitLean/Rel.lean</code>:</span>\n<div class=\"language-lean codehilite\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kd\">inductive</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Rel</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">read</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">table</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">String</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">values</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">arity</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">rows</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Relation</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">filter</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">input</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Rel</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">cond</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Expr</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">project</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">input</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Rel</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">exprs</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">List</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Expr</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">emit</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">input</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Rel</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">mapping</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">List</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">cross</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">left</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">right</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Rel</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">join</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">left</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">right</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Rel</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">JoinType</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">on</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Expr</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">\u2026</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">Read <code>| filter (input : Rel) (cond : Expr)</code> as: \"a filter is built from\nan input plan and a condition\". The recursion (a filter's input is\nitself a plan) is what makes a plan a tree.</span>\n<h2 id=\"a-plan-has-a-meaning-the-reference-interpreter\">A plan has a meaning: the reference interpreter</h2>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">To prove anything about plans, we first need to say <em>precisely</em> what each\noperator does. In Lean this is a function <code>Rel.eval</code> that takes a plan\nand the tables, and returns the result rows. It is written for clarity, not\nspeed: it is the <strong>definition</strong> of correct, against which everything else\nis measured.</span>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">Below is a line-for-line Python mirror of the Lean definitions, so you can\nsee there is no magic. (The Lean versions are in <code>SubstraitLean/Expr.lean</code>\nand <code>SubstraitLean/Rel.lean</code>.)</span></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
